WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.820
The hypothalamus has a correction to send.

2
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.440
How does it leave the brain, and how fast?

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.620
Nerves often act within seconds.

4
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.900
Hormone effects usually build more slowly, over minutes to hours.

5
00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:18.563
Start with vasopressin.

6
00:00:19.443 --> 00:00:23.603
Large neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei make it.

7
00:00:24.403 --> 00:00:28.323
Their axons release it in the posterior pituitary, straight into the blood.

8
00:00:29.316 --> 00:00:32.896
So the posterior lobe stores and releases vasopressin.

9
00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:34.616
It does not make it.

10
00:00:35.466 --> 00:00:38.086
The anterior lobe is reached by blood instead.

11
00:00:39.126 --> 00:00:42.606
Neurons release peptides into capillaries at the median eminence.

12
00:00:43.566 --> 00:00:47.326
Portal veins carry them down the stalk to a second capillary bed.

13
00:00:48.166 --> 00:00:51.946
They arrive concentrated, before the general circulation dilutes them.

14
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.220
Take the stress axis.

15
00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.960
The paraventricular nucleus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone, CRH.

16
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.480
That drives adrenocorticotropic hormone, ACTH, from the pituitary.

17
00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:09.913
ACTH drives cortisol from the adrenal cortex.

18
00:01:11.013 --> 00:01:15.513
Cortisol feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary, and turns the axis down.

19
00:01:16.516 --> 00:01:19.116
Same loop, different variable: here, a hormone.

20
00:01:20.216 --> 00:01:26.596
Now the wires. The usual autonomic chain has two neurons and two synapses: one in a

21
00:01:26.596 --> 00:01:28.156
ganglion, one on the target.

22
00:01:29.216 --> 00:01:35.696
Rule one. The first synapse is the same everywhere: acetylcholine, acting mainly on

23
00:01:35.696 --> 00:01:36.696
nicotinic receptors.

24
00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:41.950
Rule two. The second synapse usually names the division.

25
00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:46.330
Parasympathetic: acetylcholine on muscarinic receptors.

26
00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:50.510
Most sympathetic: noradrenaline on adrenergic receptors.

27
00:01:51.583 --> 00:01:53.923
Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors.

28
00:01:54.823 --> 00:01:55.823
Give a large dose.

29
00:01:56.483 --> 00:01:57.943
What happens to the heart rate?

30
00:01:58.623 --> 00:01:59.623
And to sweating?

31
00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:07.613
At a large dose, the heart rate rises.

32
00:02:08.553 --> 00:02:12.813
The vagal brake on the heart's own pacemaker was on all the time, even at rest.

33
00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:20.213
Sweating stops. The sympathetic fibres to sweat glands release acetylcholine onto

34
00:02:20.213 --> 00:02:21.293
muscarinic receptors.

35
00:02:22.173 --> 00:02:23.453
That is the first exception.

36
00:02:24.516 --> 00:02:27.476
Separate tests show steady sympathetic drive too.

37
00:02:28.336 --> 00:02:31.436
So the heart sits at a balance, like a sink filled from two taps.

38
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.120
The second exception is the adrenal medulla.

39
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.580
There, the second neuron has become a gland that releases adrenaline and

40
00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:41.820
noradrenaline into the blood.

41
00:02:42.783 --> 00:02:48.523
The paraventricular nucleus uses every route: portal blood, the posterior lobe, and

42
00:02:48.523 --> 00:02:50.003
descending autonomic pathways.

43
00:02:51.116 --> 00:02:54.676
Two routes out. Nerves often work in seconds.

44
00:02:55.596 --> 00:02:58.036
Hormone effects usually build over minutes to hours.
